  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry through \nSaturday.\n\nA split flow pattern is observed on GOES water vapor channels \nearly this morning with a northern stream in vicinity of the \nUS/Canada border and a southern stream that extends across the \nsouthern tier of the US. At the surface, 1020mb high pressure is\ncentered over the region. TD 9 is drifting slowly E in the \nwestern Gulf. Clear and cool early this morning with \ntemperatures largely in the lower to mid 40s, with 50s at the \nimmediate coast. Temperatures will still fall a few degrees over\nthe next few hours, and typically cool spots could drop into \nthe upper 30s. Patchy shallow ground fog is possible around \nsunrise, mainly in the interior coastal plain and Piedmont.\n\nHigh pressure shifts offshore Wednesday with high temperatures \nmoderating back to near average, which are in the lower to mid \n70s. However, highs across the Eastern Shore will be a little \ncooler and in the upper 60s to lower 70s. The high shifts \nfarther offshore Thursday with high temperatures rising into the\nupper 70s to lower 80s (potentially mid 80s well inland) ahead \nof another dry cold front. This cold front crosses the region \nThursday night with temperatures trending down to near seasonal \naverages Friday and Saturday. Dry conditions continue through \nSaturday. However, a gradual increase in high levels clouds is \nexpected as upper level moisture spreads over the region well in\nadvance of what is forecast to become Hurricane Isaias.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture and rain chances return late this \nweekend into early next week.\n\nBy Sunday and Monday, there is increasing confidence that \nmoisture gets pulled northward and then pushed eastward across \nthe region as the TC (forecast to become Hurricane Isaias) makes\nlandfall and merges with an upper trough in vicinity of the \nMid-South. Of the 00z/07 deterministic global models, the GFS \nand EC AIFS are more progressive showing the best rainfall \npotential in the early Sunday through Sunday evening timeframe, \nwhile the ECMWF and CMC are slower with a longer duration in the\nSunday through early Monday timeframe. Heavy rain is possible \nas ample moisture will be present, 00z ensemble systems showing \nPW values at or above 200% of normal along with a plume of \ninstability surging N ahead of the trough. There is still \nconsiderable spread between the 10th and 90th percentile QPF in \nthe 00z/07 ensemble guidance (EPS/GEFS/GEPS). In general, the \n10th percentile (reasonable low-end) accumulated precip through \n00z Tuesday is 0.25-0.5\" (hence increasing confidence of \nrainfall) while the 90th percentile is on the order of 1.5-3.0\".\nHeavy rain is possible as ample moisture will be present, \nensemble guidance showing PW values at or above 200% of normal \nalong with a plume of instability surging N ahead of the trough.\nThe overall consensus is for drier air conditions to arrive \nlater Monday and continue into the middle of next week. \nTemperatures should remain near average late this weekend \nthrough the middle of next week.",
